codomain $[0,1]?$\n\n- $p: \\{ 0, 1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $q: \\mathbb{R} \\to [ 0, 1 ]$\n\n- $r: [0,1] \\to [ 0, 1 )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/example-8474.json" "file_sha256" "1bf812c6a2c7de1b0fd2197a818662f942a01e5eb3be718dca56113509c70b00" "basis" 1355 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The notation $f: X \\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $\\mathbb{R},$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[ 0, 1 ],$ the interval from $0$ and $1$ (including $0$ and $1$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $[ 0, 1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (including $0$ but not including $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/example-8474.json" "file_sha256" "1bf812c6a2c7de1b0fd2197a818662f942a01e5eb3be718dca56113509c70b00" "basis" 1355 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$.\"*\n\n- $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\n- $g: Y \\to \\mathbb{Q}$\n\n- $g:\\mathbb{R} \\to Y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/example-8475.json" "file_sha256" "8bd5639a0f0fcc0107bac7decb4067f49a2b76bfee9167ed9c758d83b99e656e" "basis" 1355 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\qquad$ $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $\\mathbb Q,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/example-8475.json" "file_sha256" "8bd5639a0f0fcc0107bac7decb4067f49a2b76bfee9167ed9c758d83b99e656e" "basis" 1355 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x-x^3?$\n\n- $g:x \\mapsto x-x^3$\n\n- $g:x \\mapsto -x^3$\n\n- $g:-x \\mapsto x^3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/example-8476.json" "file_sha256" "8514c796d2d5d43458f9f741fb5d231195174b9221b52700d5ae813ca4a84345" "basis" 1355 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The notation $g(x)=x-x^3$ means that the function $g$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $x-x^3$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\ng:x \\mapsto x-x^3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/example-8476.json" "file_sha256" "8514c796d2d5d43458f9f741fb5d231195174b9221b52700d5ae813ca4a84345" "basis" 1355 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$\n\n- The codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- The range of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/example-8487.json" "file_sha256" "6820ad0ea265f9cbda146c145bc5bd3c4ce1b40fdd604c373d4be2574f8e754a" "basis" 1355 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The notation \n\n$$\ng: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$ to the set $\\mathbb{R}.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\},$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}.$ Note that this is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/example-8487.json" "file_sha256" "6820ad0ea265f9cbda146c145bc5bd3c4ce1b40fdd604c373d4be2574f8e754a" "basis" 1355 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-96745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following function